    "body": "Colleen Roche, Vale Base Metals’ general manager of health, safety, environment and risk in Newfoundland and Labrador (L) chats with video host Devan Murugan.\nBy: Northern Miner Staff November 19, 2026\nVale’s (NYSE: VALE) base metals unit is backing a mining engineering degree at Memorial University as it looks to recruit more skilled workers for its fly-in, fly-out Voisey’s Bay nickel mine in Labrador.\nThe company has committed $3.9 million (US$2.8 million) and will offer co-op work placements as part of a $9-million initiative with the university and the province. Memorial aims to admit its first students in fall 2027 , pending approval of the program in mining and mineral processing.\n“My life might have been very different if this program had existed 30 years ago,” Colleen Roche, Vale Base Metals’ general manager of health, safety, environment and risk in Newfoundland and Labrador, told The Northern Miner at the Mineral Resources Review in St. John’s. “I’m really excited that kids, especially kids from the Indigenous communities, will see a pathway to meaningful careers and transferable skills.”\nRoche left Newfoundland to study mining engineering at McGill University in Montreal after the 1993 Voisey’s Bay discovery. Memorial wants to train students from Indigenous and rural communities for jobs in the province.\nWatch the full interview below with The Northern Miner Video Host Devan Murugan:\nPrint\nTAG: Video\nCOMMODITY: Copper\nREGION: Canada\nCOMPANY: Vale Base Metals\nRepublish this article",
